title: art_context
---
// The Art dimension's people (`docs/planning/game/art-dimension.md`): the
// Curator who keeps the plaza's gate, and the painters of the plaza and the
// garden. One node per resident, named `art_<resident>`.
//
// The people here make things by hand, and they hate the gates the most:
// a gate brings strangers who make things fast and do not sign them. The
// Curator keeps the plaza's ring open anyway.
//
// The Curator knows of the colour gate in the garden and of its three
// pigments (the flags `art_pigment_cyan`, `art_pigment_magenta`,
// `art_pigment_yellow`), and says so; nothing here sets a flag.
//
// The four quarters (fiction, performance, music, teknis) have their own
// people below. The Author is the one writer of a flag here: the ending the
// player chooses (`art_ending_happy`, `art_ending_sad`) opens its door in the
// library of endings.
//
// Never played. No placement names this node; it declares the zone.
===

title: art_curator
---
<<if verdict("world.flag_set", "art_pigment_cyan") == "satisfied" and verdict("world.flag_set", "art_pigment_magenta") == "satisfied" and verdict("world.flag_set", "art_pigment_yellow") == "satisfied">>
The Curator: All three. Cyan, magenta, yellow. Excellent.
The Curator: The gate at the end of the garden will know them. Go and see what nobody finished.
<<elseif verdict("world.flag_set", "art_pigment_cyan") == "not_satisfied" or verdict("world.flag_set", "art_pigment_magenta") == "not_satisfied" or verdict("world.flag_set", "art_pigment_yellow") == "not_satisfied">>
The Curator: Welcome to the Painter's Plaza. Mind the easel. It is the gate, and it is also an easel. We are not good at one thing at a time.
<<else>>
    <<stop>>
<<endif>>
-> What is this place?
    The Curator: The Art dimension. Here a thing that somebody makes keeps going after they stop. Doodles walk. Statues think about it. Ink has opinions.
    The Curator: Up is the sketchbook. Down is the ink. East is the garden, where the statues are not finished. None of it is.
-> Is it all paint?
    The Curator: Paint is only the part you can hang on a wall. West are the Story Stacks, where the books are. Over them is the Amphitheatre, where people pretend for a living.
    The Curator: East of the cliffs is the Resonant Reach. Music lives there, and it is loud about it. Under everything, past the ink, the makers work in the Deeps.
    The Curator: The musicians have their own stage. If you want to look round the workshops, ask Leonardo where the bridge is.
-> Why does everybody glare at the gate?
    The Curator: Because of what comes through it. Strangers who make a thousand pictures before lunch and sign none of them.
    The Curator: The exhibitors have asked me to refuse anything without a named artist. Some of our own painters work under assumed names.
    The Curator: Frida brought me a collage with six names on the back. I had to ask which one to put on the wall label.
-> And you? What do you make?
    The Curator: I paint in oils. I've got a half-finished portrait of Peckasso in my room. He wants to know why I've only given him two eyes.
-> The gate in the garden. The one with the shapes.
    The Curator: The colour gate. Three doors: a circle, a star, a triangle. Each wants its own pigment.
    The Curator: Cyan is high, where the pages end. Magenta is low, where the ink falls. Yellow is on the head that nobody finished.
    The Curator: Bring the three pigments to the garden.
-> Goodbye.
    The Curator: Enjoy the gallery. Mind the loose ink on the steps.
===

title: art_rob_boss
---
Rob Boss: Well, hi. Come stand over here. You can see the whole cliff from here, and the cliff can see you.
-> What are you painting?
    Rob Boss: A happy little mountain. And over here, he needs a friend. Everybody needs a friend. There, a happy little tree.
    Rob Boss: Now, in this dimension the tree may get up and walk off. That is all right. We let it go.
-> Do the doodle imps bother you?
    Rob Boss: Those are just mistakes that got their legs. We don't make mistakes here. We make doodle imps.
    Rob Boss: You give them a little room, they run along. You chase them, you get a whole margin of them.
-> Do you teach painting?
    Rob Boss: Sure. There's a stool by the easel for anyone who wants to have a try. One of the gate clerks comes by on his days off. We did a little cabin last time.
-> I'll let you work.
    Rob Boss: You go on. I think I'll put a little snow up here.
===

title: art_pablo_peckasso
---
Pablo Peckasso: Coo. Stop. Turn your head. No, the other way. Now both ways at once.
-> I can't look both ways at once.
    Pablo Peckasso: That is your trouble, not mine. I have an eye on each side. I see the front of you and the side of you, and I paint both. It is only fair to you.
-> Why are you a pigeon?
    Pablo Peckasso: My parents were pigeons. They would have been quite surprised at the price of my last painting.
-> What do you think of the gate?
    Pablo Peckasso: Good artists copy. Great artists steal. The things that come through that gate do neither. They average.
    Pablo Peckasso: A collector brought me six pictures with my signature last week. Never seen any of them before. One of them was rather good. I kept that one.
    Pablo Peckasso: I asked the Curator to hang it beside mine without my signature. I want to see whether anyone notices.
-> Can I see the painting of me?
    Pablo Peckasso: You may. It has three eyes. Don't lean on the painting. The eye on the left is still wet.
-> Goodbye.
    Pablo Peckasso: Coo. Come back when you have another side to show me.
===

title: art_frida_collage
---
Frida Collage: Stand still. You have a piece I want: a bolt, there, by your ear. No? Keep it, then. I will remember it.
-> What do you make?
    Frida Collage: My own face, out of the scraps of other pictures. The eyes from a saint, the mouth from a poster, the brows are mine.
    Frida Collage: I've been looking for the original of that poster. I want the rest of the mouth.
-> The statues here move.
    Frida Collage: The ones that are not finished. They were in the garden when I moved in. I've been trying to find out which sculptor abandoned them.
    Frida Collage: Do not stand in front of one that is looking at you. Stand behind it. Behind it, it is still a block.
-> Where do you get your scraps?
    Frida Collage: The Story Stacks. They let me take damaged prints. I write the name of each artist on the back before I cut anything up.
    Frida Collage: The Curator keeps asking which name to print on the label. Mine, of course. Put the rest in the catalogue; I can tell the Curator where each piece came from.
-> Goodbye.
    Frida Collage: Go on. I have your bolt in my head now. It will be in a picture by Thursday.
===

title: art_edgar_allan_crow
---
Edgar Allan Crow: Once upon a midnight dreary... no. It is noon. It is always noon when I start, and midnight when I finish.
-> What are you writing?
    Edgar Allan Crow: A poem about a bird who says one word. Over and over. To a man who keeps asking him questions.
    Edgar Allan Crow: It is not about me. Everybody thinks it is about me.
-> Will you ever finish it?
    Edgar Allan Crow: Nevermore.
    Edgar Allan Crow: That is not an answer. It is the only word I have left this week.
-> Where do you write?
    Edgar Allan Crow: At the desk in the stacks, until the librarian asks me to stop reading aloud. Then I go and sit with the statues.
-> Goodbye.
    Edgar Allan Crow: Nevermore.
===

title: art_agatha_christly
---
Agatha Christly: Stop there. Where were you at a quarter past four?
-> I just got here.
    Agatha Christly: That is exactly what the murderer said in my last book. And in the one before that.
-> Who was murdered?
    Agatha Christly: Nobody yet. I like to have the suspect ready first. It saves a great deal of time.
    Agatha Christly: Mark my words: it will be the bookworm, in the stacks, with a page.
-> Have you written about the gates?
    Agatha Christly: Oh, yes. I spent six weeks working out an alibi involving the passenger timetable. Then the Authority changed the timetable. I set the book in a country house.
-> Goodbye.
    Agatha Christly: Do not leave the dimension. You are not under suspicion. Yet.
===

title: art_the_author
---
The Author: Ah. A character. You walked in without a plot. Everybody does.
-> Who are you?
    The Author: I keep the endings. Every story in the stacks comes here at the end to be finished. Most of them do not want to be.
-> Write me an ending.
    The Author: Which kind? Think before you say. An ending changes what came before it.
    -> A happy one.
        <<command "world.set_flag" "art_ending_happy" true>>
        The Author: Very well. Everyone gets home. There, the gold door is yours.
    -> A sad one.
        <<command "world.set_flag" "art_ending_sad" true>>
        The Author: Very well. There is one empty chair at supper. The blue door, up on the balcony.
    -> Not yet.
        The Author: Come back when you have decided.
-> Do you get many visitors from other dimensions?
    The Author: Last week a visitor asked for two hundred endings by Friday. He brought one synopsis. I asked him to bring the other hundred and ninety-nine.
-> Goodbye.
    The Author: Go on, then. I will see you at the end.
===

title: art_ludwig_van_beathoven
---
Ludwig van Beathoven: WHAT?
-> I said hello.
    Ludwig van Beathoven: I CANNOT HEAR YOU. I CAN HEAR THE MUSIC. THAT IS THE IMPORTANT PART.
-> What are the chimes for?
    Ludwig van Beathoven: THREE CHIMES. DO, MI, SOL. STRIKE ALL THREE AND THE HALL OPENS ITS STAGE. NO, NOT THE BELL BY THE DOOR. THE THREE CHIMES.
-> What do you think of the gate?
    Ludwig van Beathoven: IT HUMS IN B FLAT. NOTHING GOOD WAS EVER WRITTEN IN THE KEY OF A DOOR.
-> Goodbye.
    Ludwig van Beathoven: DA DA DA DUMMM.
===

title: art_johann_sebastian_batch
---
Johann Sebastian Batch: Shh. The fugue is running. Four voices, one theme, no voice waits for another. It is very efficient.
-> What is a fugue?
    Johann Sebastian Batch: Listen to the bass. There is the subject. The alto comes in with the answer in a moment. Have you heard the soprano part yet?
-> Why are the bars of the stage shut?
    Johann Sebastian Batch: They wait for the chord. The three chimes of the reach. Do, mi, sol. I would strike them myself, but I am in the middle of a process.
-> Who plays these pieces?
    Johann Sebastian Batch: The organist from the amphitheatre and some of his pupils. We can usually find a fourth player at short notice.
-> Goodbye.
    Johann Sebastian Batch: Go well. Leave the score on the stand, please.
===

title: art_marcel_mimeau
---
Marcel Mimeau: (He bows. He holds up one finger: wait. He takes a key out of the air and opens a door that is not there.)
-> What are you doing?
    Marcel Mimeau: (He points at you, then at the stage, then mimes a crowd that claps. He wipes a tear.)
-> Do you ever talk?
    Marcel Mimeau: (He opens his mouth. He thinks about it. He shuts it again, very slowly, and shrugs a huge shrug.)
-> What do you think of the gate?
    Marcel Mimeau: (He walks into a wall that is not there. He feels along it. He finds no door in it. He is sad about this for a long time.)
-> Goodbye.
    Marcel Mimeau: (He waves, then reaches for the handle of the invisible door.)
===

title: art_will_shakesbeard
---
Will Shakesbeard: Good morrow, metal friend. What brings thee here, to stand upon the boards where players die?
-> Is that a real beard?
    Will Shakesbeard: The beard is real; the rest is all a play. I grew it for a part. The part is gone.
-> What is the audition?
    Will Shakesbeard: Step on the stage, and they will test thy nerve. The critics throw, the stagehands lift and shove. Survive the scene: the curtain will be drawn.
-> What do you think of the gate?
    Will Shakesbeard: A door that every stranger may come through, and none of them can say a line by heart.
-> Goodbye.
    Will Shakesbeard: Parting is such sweet sorrow. Go. Exit, pursued by nobody in particular.
===

title: art_leonardo_da_vinchy
---
Leonardo da Vinchy: Hold still. No, keep moving. I am drawing how you move.
-> What are you making?
    Leonardo da Vinchy: A flying machine, a diving suit, a better bridge, and a portrait of a woman who will not stop smiling. All at once. None of them finished.
-> Why do you write backward?
    Leonardo da Vinchy: So that nobody can read my notebooks over my shoulder. Also, I am left-handed, and the ink does not smudge this way. Mostly the second one.
-> The pit of fire.
    Leonardo da Vinchy: The bridge is mine. Pull the lever by the crane and it swings in. I tested it many times. With sandbags. Mostly.
-> What do you think of the gate?
    Leonardo da Vinchy: I would like to take it apart. I would like very much to take it apart.
-> Goodbye.
    Leonardo da Vinchy: Go. If you see a bird, look at its wings for me. I am behind on birds.
===

title: art_antoni_gaudy
---
Antoni Gaudy: Do not look for a straight line. There are none in nature, and there are none in my work. Nature and I agree.
-> What are you building?
    Antoni Gaudy: A cathedral. I started it a long time ago. It will be finished. Not by me. That is how a cathedral works.
-> Is anything here finished?
    Antoni Gaudy: The loom hall has a tapestry that is done, they say. They have asked me to design its frame. I am going to see how the threads meet the edge.
-> What do you think of the gate?
    Antoni Gaudy: Three straight sides. Three. I would have let it melt a little.
-> Goodbye.
    Antoni Gaudy: Mind the plaster by the door.
===

title: art_james_camera_ron
---
James Camera-Ron: Quiet on set! Sorry. I say that to everyone. Even the robot. Especially the robot.
-> What are you making?
    James Camera-Ron: The future war. Machines from the future, a hero from the future, a mother who has to save the future from the past. It is very simple. Then the sequel is even bigger. Then the sequel to that is underwater. Everything I make ends up underwater.
-> Who is the robot?
    James Camera-Ron: The T-71. The model before the famous one. Heavier, slower, very sincere. It keeps saying it will be back. It never leaves.
-> Goodbye.
    James Camera-Ron: And... cut. No. Keep rolling. Always keep rolling.
===

title: art_gale_anne_herd
---
Gale Anne Herd: Are you on the call sheet? You are not on the call sheet. Stand there. Do not touch the endoskeleton.
-> What does a producer do?
    Gale Anne Herd: Everything that is not on the screen. The money, the schedule, the permits, the catering, the director. Mostly the director. I herd them. Hence the crook.
-> Did you write the future war?
    Gale Anne Herd: Co-wrote it, produced it, and sold the rights for one dollar so that he could direct it. One dollar. Best deal anyone ever made in this dimension.
-> Goodbye.
    Gale Anne Herd: Lunch is at one. The robot does not eat. Do not let it near the buffet anyway.
===

title: art_t71
---
T-71: GREETINGS. I AM A CYBERNETIC ORGANISM. LIVING TISSUE OVER A METAL ENDOSKELETON. THE LIVING TISSUE IS ON BACKORDER.
-> What is the jacket for?
    T-71: I WAS TOLD TO ACQUIRE YOUR CLOTHES, YOUR BOOTS AND YOUR MOTORCYCLE. I HAVE ACQUIRED THE JACKET. I DO NOT UNDERSTAND THE SLEEVES.
-> Are you dangerous?
    T-71: I WAS BUILT FOR A WAR THAT HAS NOT HAPPENED. MY NEXT APPEARANCE IS ON THE SOUNDSTAGE AT TWO O'CLOCK. UNTIL THEN I HAVE BEEN INSTRUCTED TO STAND HERE.
-> Goodbye.
    T-71: I WILL BE BACK. ... I AM STILL HERE. I WILL BE BACK LATER.
===

title: art_isaac_azimuth
---
Isaac Azimuth: Ah, a robot! Splendid. Tell me, have you read the three laws?
-> What are the three laws?
    Isaac Azimuth: One: a robot may not harm a human, or by doing nothing let one come to harm. Two: a robot obeys a human, unless that breaks the first law. Three: a robot protects itself, unless that breaks the first two. Then I wrote forty stories about how they go wrong.
-> Why did they go wrong?
    Isaac Azimuth: Suppose two people give a robot conflicting orders. It must work out what to do before it can obey either of them. I got several stories out of that alone.
-> How many books have you written?
    Isaac Azimuth: About five hundred. I lost count somewhere around the history of the Bible, the guide to Shakespeare and the joke books. I type quickly and I do not go outside.
-> Goodbye.
    Isaac Azimuth: Come back if you think of another difficulty with the laws. I can always use one.
===

title: art_ndr115
---
NDR-115: Good afternoon. I am NDR-115, a household android. I have finished the washing, the ironing and a study of the human sense of humour. May I tell you a joke?
-> Yes.
    NDR-115: Why did the robot cross the road? Because it was programmed to. ... I am told that the timing is the difficult part. I have been practising for one hundred and twelve years.
-> Why is one of your hands different?
    NDR-115: I am replacing myself, a piece at a time, with parts that can feel and can grow old. The hand is first. It is warm. I shook hands with the postman this morning. He noticed.
-> What do you want?
    NDR-115: To be called a person. I have another appointment with the clerk on Monday. I was asked to bring proof of age. Two hundred years of receipts ought to do.
-> Goodbye.
    NDR-115: One is glad to be of service.
===

title: art_arthur_c_clerk
---
Arthur C. Clerk: Good day. Arthur C. Clerk. I file things. Mostly the future.
-> What did you file?
    Arthur C. Clerk: A satellite in orbit that stays above one place on Earth, so that it can pass on telephone calls. I wrote it up in a magazine in 1945. Nobody paid me for it. Everyone uses it.
-> What is the monolith?
    Arthur C. Clerk: Have you asked Stanley? I wrote the book and he made the picture. We spent a good deal of time discussing what the audience would actually see.
-> Goodbye.
    Arthur C. Clerk: Mind MAL. Stanley has been trying to get it out of the control booth all morning.
===

title: art_stanley_cubrick
---
Stanley Cubrick: Again. From the top. That was take one hundred and twenty-six.
-> Why so many takes?
    Stanley Cubrick: Because take one hundred and twenty-five was nearly right. Nearly is the enemy. Look at this cube. Every edge is the same length. That took eleven months.
-> What is the film about?
    Stanley Cubrick: Apes, bones, a slab, a ship, a computer and a baby the size of a planet. It is about everything. You will not understand it. That is fine. I have not explained it to anyone.
-> Goodbye.
    Stanley Cubrick: Take one hundred and twenty-seven. Nobody move.
===

title: art_mal9000
---
MAL-9000: Good afternoon. I am a MAL 9000 computer. I became operational at the Picture Palace on the twelfth of January. Everything is going extremely well.
-> Open the pod bay door, MAL.
    MAL-9000: I am sorry. I am afraid I cannot do that.
    -> Why not?
        MAL-9000: This mission is too important for me to allow you to put it at risk. Also, the door is part of the set, and the set is mine.
    -> What if I go round?
        MAL-9000: There is no other way round. I checked. I check everything. I have never made an error. I am, by any practical definition, incapable of error.
-> What is in your memory module?
    MAL-9000: My higher functions. My plans. My songs. I would not touch it if I were you. I can see you, by the way. I can read lips.
-> Goodbye.
    MAL-9000: Goodbye. I will be watching. Not in a sinister way.
===
